--TEST--
application code and the engine floor produce schema-identical records for the same error — `rule:errors/panics-bypass-user-code` and `rule:errors/log-write`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:errors/log-write`'s load-bearing claim, asserted as *agreement* rather than as
// two independent shapes: `Core\Log::write` is a thin binding over the same
// native record-and-write helper tier 4 calls directly when it has no script
// left to run, so a Loki dashboard never has to reconcile two log shapes
// depending on which tier produced a given line.
//
// The two lines below are the same error reported from the two ends of the
// ladder. They carry the same envelope keys in the same order, each field
// nested the same way. Two keys are one writer's and not the other's, and each
// is that writer having a source for it: the application's line names where it
// was written (`rule:errors/a-record-names-where-it-was-produced`, which the
// floor has no call site for), and the floor's carries the frames this caller
// has none of — one `nodes` entry per frame, which is
// `rule:errors/record-producers`'s shape for the throw producer. The channel
// is the same too: with no `[log] target` both writers write standard error,
// so the two lines follow each other there and standard output stays empty.
Core\Log::write(Core\Log::Level::Error, "boom", ["class" => "RuntimeError"]);

throw new RuntimeError("boom");
--EXPECT--
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
{"level":"error","msg":"boom","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":18},"fields":{"class":"RuntimeError"}}
{"level":"error","msg":"boom","fields":{"class":"RuntimeError"},"nodes":[{"function":"<script>","file":"case.nvs","line":%d}]}
